  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 150 PM MDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nIt continues to look like TS Rachel will be sheared apart and the\nmoisture remnants will be absorbed into a Pacific low west of the\nBaja on Saturday. Then those moisture remnants will shift into\nSoCal and SW AZ Sunday. While Rachel stays well west of NM, PWATs\nwill increase over NM this weekend as southwest flow aloft \nincreases. PWATs around 0.50\" on Friday afternoon should rise \ntoward 0.75-0.80\" along and west of the Rio Grande Valley on \nSaturday, and between 0.75-1.00\" areawide on Sunday. Precipitation\nwill favor the western third of NM each day, but a few showers \nand storms could reach as far east as the Central Mtn Chain. \nThrough the weekend, most areas should pick up less than 0.25\" \nthough more persistent showers or isolated thunderstorms could \nbring a few areas up to 1 inch of rainfall. Given the stronger \nsouthwest flow aloft, orographic lift will be a big player \ndictating what areas will be favored for higher precipitation\namounts. At this time, it appears the Southwest Mountains and \nTusas Mountains will be favored.\n\nA deeper lee side trough should develop Saturday, which will bring\nwind gusts around 35 mph to northeast NM once again, though a few\nbreezes will be felt elsewhere. Winds aloft increase from around \n15-20kt on Saturday to 20-35kt on Sunday at H7. Modest mixing\ncombined with another lee side surface trough will allow gusts \nbetween 25-35 mph areawide Sunday aftn.\n\nTS Simon will continue to strengthen south of Mexico before\nshifting northward and making landfall in MX late Sunday. Where\nthe remnants of Simon will go thereafter will depend on the\nprogression of the deep upper low diving southward along the west\ncoast on Sunday. The latest operational GFS and EC show the low \nejecting eastward faster than previous model runs, which helps \ndirect the moisture from Simon away from NM and perhaps only \nclipping SE or eastern areas of the state. Now models are\nindicating that a dry slot will punch into NM and winds will \nremain breezy. Therefore, both the operational GFS and EC are \nconsiderably drier for NM for Monday night through Wednesday. This\nalso means they have trended toward the AI and Ensemble \nforecasts. Given the AI models have been consistently drier and \nensembles have consistently shown PWATs staying at or below 0.75\" \nfor all but eastern NM, it seems unlikely that operational models \nwill flip-flop back to a wetter solution. Scattered showers and \nthunderstorms remain forecast for the period in case, but if this \ntrend continues, PoPs may be reduced in the future. \n\nThe upper low still looks to track eastward over or near the Four\nCorners on Sunday, though perhaps a weaker low than what was\nthought previously. Some scattered showers and thunderstorms may\nstill impact northwest portions of the state on Wednesday, but the\ndry slot looks to be firmly in place elsewhere.",
